HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the standard markup language used to create and design the structure of web pages. It is one of the core technologies of web development and acts as the foundation for creating websites and web applications. HTML uses a system of tags and elements to organize content such as headings, paragraphs, images, links, tables, forms, audio, and videos on a webpage.

This repository contains various HTML programs, mini projects, and webpage designs developed for learning and practicing front-end web development concepts. The projects included in this repository demonstrate the use of basic and advanced HTML elements, semantic tags, forms, tables, lists, multimedia integration, and webpage layouts. It also includes examples of responsive webpage structures that help improve understanding of modern web design practices.

HTML is easy to learn, platform-independent, and supported by all web browsers. It works together with CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) for styling and JavaScript for adding interactivity and dynamic behavior to web pages. By combining these technologies, developers can create visually appealing and user-friendly websites.

The purpose of this repository is to strengthen web development skills through practical implementation of HTML concepts. The projects are designed to help beginners understand webpage structure, document formatting, hyperlinks, navigation bars, forms, and semantic elements used in real-world applications. Each program is written in a simple and understandable format, making it useful for students, beginners, and anyone interested in learning web development.

This repository can also serve as a reference for academic projects, interview preparation, and portfolio development. It reflects problem-solving skills, creativity, and the ability to build structured web pages using clean and organized code. Future improvements may include adding CSS styling, JavaScript functionality, responsive designs, and full-stack integration to create complete web applications.

Overall, this repository represents a strong foundation in HTML and front-end development, showcasing continuous learning, hands-on practice, and interest in building modern and interactive websites.
